<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orăștie Mountains</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Orăștie Mountains are a range of the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> in Hunedoara County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, south of the town of Orăștie. They were the heart of the Dacian kingdom from the 1st century BCE to 106 CE, with a ring of fortresses, among them Costești, Blidaru and Piatra Roșie, protecting the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmizegetusa-regia">Sarmizegetusa Regia</a>. Six of these sites form a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/orastie-mountains.jpg" alt="Orăștie Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oroles · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sanctuarele_de_andezit.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Dacian fortresses of the Orăștie Mountains were built in the 1st centuries BCE and CE in the murus dacicus style, as protection against Roman conquest, and they were important in the Roman–Dacian wars. Six of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmizegetusa-regia">Sarmizegetusa Regia</a>, Costești-Cetățuie, Costești-Blidaru, Piatra Roșie, Bănița and Căpâlna, formed the defensive system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decebalus">Decebalus</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarmizegetusa-regia">Sarmizegetusa Regia</a> was the capital and major fortress of the Dacian kingdom, probably built in the mid first century BCE. It stood at the top of a hill 1,200 metres high with excellent visibility of the surrounding lands. It consisted of perimeter walls and fortifications, a sacred precinct and a settlement area primarily for nobles and their servants. The sacred precinct lay on the east side, with a prominent plaza and circular shrines. There were two settlement areas, one on the east side and a larger one on the west, with dwellings, workshops, storage buildings and areas for processing farm produce. A distribution system for drinking water using ceramic pipes was notable for its time.</p><p>Piatra Roșie, meaning Red Rock, was a hill fort two days' march west of Costești-Cetățuie, at Luncani in Boșorod commune. It was built in two phases. First came a rectangular main citadel 102 metres long on the height of land, with watch towers at each end and two outlying towers. Later the larger area inside the watch towers was enclosed with walls, and the hilltop appears to have been flattened to make usable space.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The six fortresses were designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1999. All are in Hunedoara County except Căpâlna, which is in Alba County. Their extensive and well-preserved remains present a picture of a vigorous and innovative ancient civilisation. Treasure-hunters sometimes search the area.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dacian Fortresses of the Orăștie Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The heart of the kingdom lay in the <b>Orăștie Mountains</b>, where fortresses and sanctuaries ringed the capital, Sarmizegetusa Regia.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-dacia">Kingdom of Dacia</a></li></ul></section>
